<!-- EXPLAINERS -->
<section id="explainers">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">07 · Explainer Lab</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">How it actually works</h2>
    <p class="section-sub">Short, structured breakdowns of systems people use every day. Click to expand.</p>
    <div class="explainer-list">
      <div class="glass explainer-row" onclick="this.classList.toggle('open')">
        <span class="exq">How ChatGPT works</span><span class="exarrow">›</span>
      </div>
      <div class="explainer-answer">
        A prompt is broken into tokens, passed through a transformer network that uses attention to weigh relationships between tokens, and the model outputs a probability distribution over the next token — repeated until a full response is generated.
        <div class="flow mono">
          <span>Prompt</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Tokenize</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Transformer</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Attention</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Next-token probabilities</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Response</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="glass explainer-row" onclick="this.classList.toggle('open')">
        <span class="exq">How neural networks learn</span><span class="exarrow">›</span>
      </div>
      <div class="explainer-answer">
        A network makes a prediction, compares it to the true answer with a loss function, and backpropagation computes how much each weight contributed to the error — then gradient descent nudges every weight slightly to reduce that error, repeated over many examples.
        <div class="flow mono"><span>Forward pass</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Loss</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Backpropagation</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Gradient descent</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Updated weights</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="glass explainer-row" onclick="this.classList.toggle('open')">
        <span class="exq">How recommendation systems work</span><span class="exarrow">›</span>
      </div>
      <div class="explainer-answer">
        Systems like YouTube or Netflix combine collaborative signals (what similar users watched) with content signals (what the item is about), then rank candidates by predicted engagement for that specific user.
        <div class="flow mono"><span>User history</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Candidate generation</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Ranking model</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Personalized feed</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="glass explainer-row" onclick="this.classList.toggle('open')">
        <span class="exq">How cloud computing works</span><span class="exarrow">›</span>
      </div>
      <div class="explainer-answer">
        Instead of owning physical servers, you rent compute, storage, and networking from a provider's data centers, provisioned on demand through APIs, and billed by usage rather than ownership.
        <div class="flow mono"><span>Request</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Provider API</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Virtualized resources</span><span class="arrow">→</span><span>Data center hardware</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

